        "math_academy_id": "q-103243",
        "problem": "If $cos\u2061x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ then $cos\u2061(x-14\\pi )=$",
        "worked_solution": "The period of $y=cos\u2061x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncos\u2061x=cos\u2061(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-7$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos\u2061x=cos\u2061(x+2\\cdot (-7)\\cdot \\pi )=cos\u2061(x-14\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cos\u2061x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ then $cos\u2061(x-14\\pi )=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$",
